# Sources for Court intervention in the Sixteenth-Majles election and the
# opposition's constitutional response: Fakhreddin Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of
# Democracy, 1941–1953, pp. 201–208; Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two
# Revolutions, ch. 5, pp. 249–267. The particular reactions and effects below
# are counterfactual scenario responses, not undocumented historical acts.
@crown_pressure_response
title: Pressure from the <span class="term term-royalist">Court</span>
subtitle: Royal influence turns an opposition initiative into a test of access, procedure, and organization
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: crown_reaction_due = 1
priority: 15
on-arrival: {!
Q.crown_reaction_due = 0;
Q.last_crown_reaction_time = Q.time;
if (!Q.majles_convened) {
    Q.election_project_setbacks += 1;
    Q.last_project_setback = "Court pressure on constituency work";
} else {
    Q.oil_project_setbacks += 1;
    Q.last_project_setback = "Court pressure on parliamentary work";
}
!}

The {!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} has the confidence
and connections to resist the opposition's latest advance.
[? if majles_convened = 0: Local correspondents report that official access
and electoral influence are making several contests harder to defend. ?]
[? if majles_convened = 1: Deputies and intermediaries find that access,
scheduling, and private pressure now work against the opposition's
parliamentary case. ?]

[? if shah_relation < 35: No regular channel to the
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Shah{!</span>!} can be taken for
granted. ?]
[? if shah_relation >= 35: A guarded channel to the palace remains open, but
using it risks appearing to bargain away the public constitutional case. ?]

The opposition must decide whether to answer through access, public
constitutional argument, or direct protection of the records under pressure.

- @crown_seek_access
- @crown_public_record
- @crown_defend_places

@crown_seek_access
title: Use the guarded palace channel
subtitle: A private constitutional warning may contain resistance if the relationship can still bear it
choose-if: shah_relation >= 35
unavailable-subtitle: Relations with the <span class="term term-royalist">Crown</span> are too hostile for a useful approach.
on-arrival: shah_relation += 3; shah_resistance -= 3; constitutional_legitimacy += 1; last_reaction = "Guarded palace approach"

The approach is formal and narrow. It asks the palace to avoid making itself
the visible author of administrative obstruction and leaves the opposition's
substantive program outside the conversation.

The channel remains guarded, but immediate resistance eases. Allies who wanted
a public confrontation receive no dramatic declaration.

- @post_event: Continue

@crown_public_record
title: Make the pressure a constitutional question
subtitle: A public warning denies the <span class="term term-royalist">Court</span> quiet influence but hardens the confrontation
on-arrival: constitutional_legitimacy += 3; press_capacity += 2; shah_resistance += 2; last_reaction = "Public constitutional warning"

Friendly papers describe the institutional pressure precisely and ask which
authority is responsible. They do not claim knowledge they lack or turn every
adverse decision into a palace order.

The public case becomes clearer. The
{!<span class="term term-royalist">!}Court{!</span>!} also has less room to retreat
without appearing to yield to the opposition.

- @post_event: Continue

@crown_defend_places
title: Reinforce the contested records
subtitle: One resource keeps lawyers and organizers answering influence with files, witnesses, and sustained local work
choose-if: resources >= 1 and majles_convened = 0
unavailable-subtitle: Requires one resource and an election still in progress.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
for (var place of Q.majles_places) {
    if (place.scenario.contestable) {
        place.scenario.legal_defense += 5;
        place.scenario.campaign_investment += 3;
    }
}
Q.last_reaction = "Reinforced contested records";
!}

Money goes to travel, copies, witness statements, and the organizers keeping
each local record alive. The work cannot eliminate official influence, but it
makes a disputed return harder to dispose of without argument.

- @post_event: Continue

# Source for the Front's heterogeneous structure and recurring internal
# tensions: Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, ch. 5, pp. 250–267.
# The timing and particular dispute are counterfactual.
@coalition_strain
title: A dispute inside the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span>
subtitle: A common campaign has outrun the agreement that was supposed to hold it together
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: front_formed = 1 and coalition_cohesion < 45 and time - last_coalition_reaction_time >= 4
priority: 12
on-arrival: last_coalition_reaction_time = time; coalition_procedure_setbacks += 1; last_project_setback = "Coalition procedure challenged"

The component organizations have carried the same public demand for several
months, but they no longer agree on who may speak for the whole coalition.
[? if coalition_procedure = "Unsettled": Meetings have produced decisions
without settling how those decisions bind the participants. ?]
[? if coalition_procedure != "Unsettled": The existing “[+
coalition_procedure +]” procedure is now being tested by a disagreement it did
not prevent. ?]

Continuing without a settlement preserves the immediate campaign. Reopening
the coalition's procedure will determine whose consent the next common action
requires.

No component can be ordered into agreement. The available choices redistribute
authority and delay rather than making disagreement disappear.

- @strain_narrow
- @strain_council
- @strain_defer

@strain_narrow
title: Reduce the common line to one constitutional demand
subtitle: A narrower obligation protects the alliance at the price of abandoning work some allies expected
on-arrival: coalition_procedure = "Narrow common mandate"; coalition_procedure_stage += 1; iran_party_relation += 3; independent_nationalists_relation += 3; toilers_dissent += 2; religious_network_dissent += 1; last_reaction = "Coalition mandate narrowed"

The meeting removes disputed social and organizational language from the
common resolution. {!<span class="term term-constitutionalist">!}Iran
Party{!</span>!} figures and independent constitutionalists can
defend what remains, while other organizers record what has been postponed.

- @post_event: Continue

@strain_council
title: Give every component a voice in a standing council
subtitle: Consultation slows the coalition but makes unilateral action harder
on-arrival: coalition_procedure = "Component council"; coalition_procedure_stage += 1; iran_party_dissent -= 2; toilers_dissent -= 2; independent_nationalists_dissent -= 2; religious_network_dissent -= 2; organizational_reach -= 1; last_reaction = "Component council convened"

Each component names its position before a common statement is released.
Decisions take longer, but allies are less likely to discover a coalition line
after it has already appeared in print.

- @post_event: Continue

@strain_defer
title: Leave the disagreement unresolved
subtitle: Immediate work continues while each organization carries its grievance back to its own supporters
on-arrival: public_mandate += 2; iran_party_dissent += 2; toilers_dissent += 2; independent_nationalists_dissent += 2; religious_network_dissent += 2; last_reaction = "Coalition dispute deferred"

The public campaign proceeds without a settlement. That preserves the current
tempo, but it also lets every component explain the dispute in its own way.

- @post_event: Continue
